Consider:

I come up to you, holding out a rock in one hand, and I ask, “Do you know what I have here?” To which, you reply, reasonably: “It’s a rock.” To which I reply: “Ah-ah. Its’s two rocks.” It looks for all the world like a single solid, rock, so unperturbed, you ask: “Where’s the break in it?” To which I reply: “There is no break.” Your reply: “Then how are there two rocks?” My response: “Ah, well… these two rocks you see, they are identical.” You: “But you clearly have only one rock.” Me: “No, I have two—they just happen to be identical… in every way, down to the subatomic particle… they are so identical, in fact, that they share even the same physical space and temporal extent.” You: “Let me hold it.” [I oblige, and you heft it for weight] “Feels like an ordinary rock to me.” Me: “As I said, these two rocks are completely identical, they are made of the same atoms and molecules, each in the same place as the other, these two rocks are identical in absolutely every way.” You: “Then how do you know there are two rocks?” Me: “Because one is Rock A, and the other, Rock B.” You: “Then how do you tell them apart? Is there some test you can perform?” Me: “If there were, they wouldn’t be identical, would they?” You: “Then there is one rock, you just have two labels for it.” Me: “No, one is distinctly Rock A, the other quite clearly Rock B.” We’ll cut this scenario short because it goes nowhere.

…but: This is quixotic same predicament is what we get into whenever we assert the fact of any matter that extends beyond what can be observed. Whether it’s a scientific theory or a fact of existence. So long as each proposition accounts for all possible observations… it is equivalent to any other that does the same, and the proposition collapses. You could explain Newton’s gravity, or Einstein’s, and I could propose exactly the opposite laws—gravity is repulsive, it increases with distance and decreases with mass, etc… we stick to earth because it’s the least distant, greatest mass object, thus the rest of the universe is repelling us more effectively… etc. Or I could come up with an extravagant, highly baroque gravity based on the will and activity of sub-femtometer scale gnomes, and—so long as I elaborated it sufficiently to account for every observation (and more to the point, every concievable observation, and every conceivable prediction, and so long as all the outcomes are identical), it would therefore be entirely equivalent to any equally predictive theory. Moreover, anything at all beyond what is currently available to my senses falls into that same category: equivalent collapse. Solipsism? Realism? Collapse. Agency? Determinism? Collapse. Other minds? Collapse. Is memory a recording of the past or an hallucination in the moment? Collapse. Is this glowing pattern of lights before me an iPhone upon which I’m typing a philosophical conundrum or just an illusion? Collapse. All theories become a matter of pragmatic usefulness or aesthetic choice.

